WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.542 --> 00:00:03.125
(upbeat music)

00:00:14.823 --> 00:00:17.050
- Well, I think what our
new framework is going to do

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.850
is it's going to make it more likely

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.720
that people at the margins of society

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.010
can participate in our labor market.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:26.570
We're not going to preemptively

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:27.950
start raising interest rates

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:30.800
when we see unemployment
getting extremely low,

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.070
unless we see signs of inflation.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.800
And I think what that will
do is mean that the economy

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.430
will have some extended stimulus

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.930
and that will allow
more people to get jobs

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:43.580
for a much longer period of time.

00:00:44.551 --> 00:00:48.000
(upbeat music)

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.130
By the Federal Reserve Act,

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:51.370
The Federal Reserve has two mandates.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:52.910
One is stable prices,

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.600
to make sure that the price level

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.550
does not fluctuate too wildly.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.080
And the other is maximum employment,

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.130
to try to make sure that the economy

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:03.730
is operating as robustly
and as large as possible.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.350
We work to try to manage that

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.710
through an interest rate policy,

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:12.183
as well as other tools that
we have at our disposal.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.360
The Phillips Curve is
the relationship between

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.530
employment and the level of prices.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.550
So what the Phillips Curve says is that,

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:27.020
as we get employment and labor markets

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.260
that are tighter and tighter,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.460
inflation will get more and more intense.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.010
And so as you get closer and closer

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:34.460
to full employment levels,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.080
you should see according to the curve

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.890
spikes in inflation that
could move the economy

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.881
into a much less stable position

00:01:40.881 --> 00:01:43.260
when it comes to the price level.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.190
So what the Phillips Curve
tells us about employment

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.690
is that as levels of employment go up,

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:52.020
as you get to labor markets
that are tighter and tighter

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.830
that's going to put pressure on wages,

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:57.540
firms are going to have to
compete more to get workers,

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.310
and that will translate into
higher prices for final goods.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.580
High inflation in the 1970s

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.890
was really detrimental to the economy.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.640
- The tyranny of double digit inflation

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:12.743
is our common enemy in 1974.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.530
- The experience in the seventies

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.420
certainly was a a marker

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.510
and it allowed The Fed to really reflect

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.810
and come to a conclusion that
they didn't want to have that

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.510
ever happen again.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.540
And so there is a propensity
and has been a propensity

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.720
to proactively prevent

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.173
inflation from spiking
to very high levels.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.240
When people say the Phillips
Curve has flattened,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:46.290
what they mean is that

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.060
we're not seeing the same type of response

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:52.260
in the price level to very
high levels of employment

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.310
that we used to.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:57.145
So that sensitivity to tight labor markets

00:02:57.145 --> 00:03:00.354
is not triggering inflation
in the marketplace.

00:03:00.354 --> 00:03:03.128
And to that extent, what it means is that

00:03:03.128 --> 00:03:05.460
if you drew that curve out,

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.560
it would show a much flatter relationship.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:11.153
(upbeat music)

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.650
What we see in terms of our
general employment levels

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.710
in this many of the national statistics

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:29.960
are really averages of a number
of different relationships.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.772
And so we actually try to
dive more deeply into that

00:03:33.772 --> 00:03:37.730
and see how different segments
of the economy are performing

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.240
and how workers from different
experiences and backgrounds

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.740
are experiencing the economy.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.470
The data has been pretty clear
on this for quite some time

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.640
that the employment for
minorities has lagged

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.650
that for the general population.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:53.350
And if you look at unemployment rates,

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:56.110
I think the basic rule has been that

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:58.630
unemployment rates for African Americans

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:02.663
has been about twice that
for the general population.

00:04:14.802 --> 00:04:17.290
Well, the natural rate of
unemployment is basically

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.540
the level of unemployment
that you would see

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.110
if everyone who wanted
a job could have one

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.900
and it was not going to trigger inflation.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.620
My estimate of the
natural rate of employment

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:29.780
has really changed over my entire career.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.380
When I started in my role here,

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:35.670
the estimate was more in the
four and a half percent range.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.910
I've really perceived that the

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.970
natural rate has fallen even further,

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:44.970
somewhere south of 4%, maybe
in the 3.7 to 3.8% level.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.760
And that's really informed
how I've thought about

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.813
what appropriate policy should look like.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.400
Well, I think we've learned a lot

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:57.280
over the last seven or eight years

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.530
since the great financial crisis,

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:01.760
and all of the signals that we've learned

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:05.460
have suggested that we
can be a bit more patient

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:07.990
in increasing rates and responding

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:09.700
to very tight labor markets.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:10.900
So I think moving forward,

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:12.150
what we will expect

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.410
and what I expect we'll
see from the committee

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:18.740
is a willingness to keep
rates lower for longer,

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.069
and really wait for signs of inflation,

00:05:21.069 --> 00:05:25.533
before moving to a more
proactive policy approach.

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:34.160
The higher inflation is
a challenge for people

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.140
that have constrained incomes
and constrained wealth.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.740
And this is one of the reasons why

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:42.150
as we think about what
appropriate policy is

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:44.840
we keep those people in
mind and make sure that

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.030
we are doing all we can

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:48.800
to understand the
challenges that they face.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.360
But one thing I would say is that,

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.640
if we're going to have an
economy that works for all people

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.640
monetary policy is just going
to be one tool for that.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.810
We're going need investments
on the fiscal side,

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:02.520
as well as in state and local governments

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.660
to make sure that there's
an infrastructure,

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:07.178
so that people can get the skills

00:06:07.178 --> 00:06:10.020
that allow them to have higher wages

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.140
so they can manage and prosper

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:14.063
in an economy that's growing robustly.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.393
(upbeat music)

